    "notes": "Recorded from `codex app-server` 0.150.1 driven live over stdio JSON-RPC against a disposable git repository under TMPDIR, never the AutoStack checkout. Machine-specific values are normalized to TRANSCRIPT_PLACEHOLDERS in both the frames and the recorded argv: the temp repository to /tmp/agent-workspace, the home directory to /home/agent, the username to agent. `-c mcp_servers={}` is part of every recorded argv because the operator's own MCP servers otherwise spam `mcpServer/startupStatus/updated` notifications and stderr auth failures into the transcript; it is the Codex analogue of Claude's `--setting-sources project`. WIRE SHAPE, VERBATIM: Codex app-server responses and notifications carry NO `jsonrpc` member -- responses are {\"id\":n,\"result\":{...}} and notifications are {\"method\":...,\"params\":...,\"emittedAtMs\":n}. Only the CLIENT's outbound messages carry \"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\" in the recorded driver. A parser that requires `jsonrpc` on inbound frames will reject every real Codex frame. `initialize` returns {userAgent, codexHome, platformFamily, platformOs} and negotiates NOTHING: unlike ACP, Codex advertises no capabilities, so the Codex descriptor must be derived from the launch profile (app-server versus exec) rather than negotiated. `turn/completed` is a TURN boundary, not a session boundary -- `completed` is minted on session end, exactly as for Claude's `result`. Response ids are as recorded (initialize 0, thread/start 1, turn/start 2), so a replaying client must mint request ids in the same order; the awaitStdin matches are structural subsets on `method`, so they do not pin ids the client mints. MALFORMED REPRESENTATION: this is the `completes` flow with exactly one frame corrupted -- the `item/completed` agentMessage notification, replaced by a truncated copy of its own first eighty bytes. The transcript format's `emit` frame is serialized by the fixture provider as one JSON value per line, so a genuinely unparseable byte sequence cannot be expressed directly; the closest faithful representation the format supports is an `emit` whose value is that truncated line as a JSON STRING. The provider therefore writes a well-formed JSON line that is not a Codex app-server message, which is exactly where `provider_output_malformed` must be classified -- a classified failure, never a crash, and never provider text leaking into the error. An `emitStderr` diagnostic accompanies it because stderr bytes must be proved never to reach the frame parser. Every other frame is byte-identical to codex-completes.json."
  },
